# Saphira 0.2.1 base system, OpenRC target (product: Saphira).
#
# Intentions ONLY. The package manager owns dependency closure; never
# enumerate resolved dependencies here (saphira-profiles(5)). Anything
# saphira-base already owns (bash, ca-certificates, coreutils, musl,
# accounts/shadow, baselayout, apk-tools, util-linux) is deliberately
# NOT repeated below.
#
# The kernel is pinned by LINE, not by version: saphira-kernel-7.2.3 is
# a per-kver co-installable identity, so naming it is a bare-package
# intention (recipe-rules.sh compliant) that still selects the known-good
# 7.2.3 line.
#
# ZFS is NOT in a clean image. It was here in r14 and it was wrong: a
# base Saphira image carries no filesystem driver the distribution does
# not use, and ZFS on a root filesystem is a deliberate flavour
# decision - root on ZFS, built and shipped as such - not something a
# clean image happens to acquire because a kernel module package was
# convenient to depend on. The saphira-zfs-* recipes stay in the
# catalogue for that flavour; they are simply not seeded here.
#
# Product identity is package-owned: saphira-release ships
# /etc/os-release identifying Saphira Linux 0.2.1 (see the release
# package spec in the 0.2.1 plan). The systemd twin of this profile
# names saphirad-release instead; the init choice is therefore visible
# inside the running system, not just in build data.
#
# Each entry earns its place:
#
#   saphira-base        libc/loader, shell (bash), core runtime, account
#                       and package tooling, CA trust, FHS/permissions.
#                       The foundation; never re-enumerated.
#   saphira-kernel-7.2.3
#                       the kernel and its modules, 7.2.3 line. Boot is
#                       impossible without it.
#   saphira-release     product identity (Saphira Linux 0.2.1).
#   openrc              the init system for this target.
#   grub                the bootloader. Deliberately explicit: a bootable
#                       image carries its own bootloader, and later kernel
#                       upgrades need it.
#   e2fsprogs           fsck.ext4/mkfs.ext4 for the ext4 root filesystem.
#   openssh             remote administration, client and server.
#   saphira-network     the supported network-configuration framework
#                       (static per-interface files, DHCP when configured).
#                       Owns the iproute2 dependency, so iproute2 is not
#                       named here.
#   dhcpcd              DHCP client, used by saphira-network only when a
#                       config selects DHCP. The boot-test network is DHCP.
#   nftables            firewalling.
#   acpid               ACPI power-button handling (virsh shutdown and
#                       the physical button power the machine off
#                       cleanly; without it the guest only halts).
#   vim                 the base editor.
#   iputils             ping and friends. A base host with no ping is not
#                       administrable; proposed explicitly, kept deliberately.
#   tzdata              a server with no timezone cannot reason about
#                       logs, certificates or scheduling.
#
# Deliberately absent, each a decision rather than an oversight:
#   - chrony (time sync is a service/role, not a base prerequisite);
#   - bind (running DNS services are a role; resolver support is musl
#     plus saphira-network-written resolv.conf, already covered);
#   - dosfstools (vfat is a UEFI/ESP need; the 0.2.1 BIOS seed carries
#     no FAT filesystem. UEFI profiles add it back);
#   - ca-certificates (already inside saphira-base);
#   - iproute2 (owned by the saphira-network dependency);
#   - a graphical stack of any kind (see the headless policy);
#   - compilers, headers, *-dev, Perl, Python, PHP, databases,
#     QEMU/libvirt, saphira-tools, install/image tooling: builder-side
#     or role material, never base.
saphira-base
saphira-kernel-7.2.3
saphira-release
openrc
grub
e2fsprogs
openssh
saphira-network
dhcpcd
nftables
acpid
vim
iputils
tzdata
